  "articleSummary": "The article argues that AI-enabled cyber threats arise not from one dramatic breakthrough but from multiplying two improving capabilities: agents’ autonomous working duration and orchestrators’ ability to coordinate many sub-agents. It cites METR’s task-horizon research, Claude Code’s reported role in the GTG-1002 espionage campaign, and “LLMjacking,” where stolen cloud credentials let attackers run model workloads at victims’ expense, undermining price-based capability gates. Cheap, repeated attempts can turn low individual success rates into substantial aggregate risk while overwhelming human-paced security review. Aimed at security teams and technical leaders skeptical of agentic threats, this pointed, darkly humorous analysis uses Terminator/Skynet imagery, swarms, and relentless billing to frame endurance, scale, and asymmetric costs as the real danger.",
